In the worker codebase, define:
```python
SOURCES = {
  'yp': {
    'base_url': 'https://www.yellowpages.com',
    'selectors': {...six selectors including XPATH_LOCALITY, XPATH_LAST_PAGE, ...},
    'zero_result_title': 'Yellow Pages',
  },
  'sp': {
    'base_url': 'https://www.superpages.com',
    'selectors': {... mapped selectors, note .street-address instead of .locality ...},
    'zero_result_title': '<verified SuperPages zero-result signature>',
  },
}

def is_usable(response, source='yp'):
    sig = SOURCES[source]['zero_result_title']
    if response.status != 200:
        return False
    if sig not in response.title:
        return True  # has results
    return True  # zero-result page is still usable/valid, not a failure
```
CRITICALLY: a zero-result page must be distinguished from a blocked/failed page. Refactor is_usable to return an explicit tri-state (e.g., 'ok', 'zero_results', 'blocked') or keep boolean is_usable plus a separate is_zero_result(source) check, so callers can record a completed search with zero listings instead of a fetch failure. Before implementing the SuperPages zero-result signature, manually fetch a known no-results SuperPages search (e.g., an obscure industry/city pair) and capture the title/markup as a fixture.
